{"step_index":1468,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:53:51Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:53:51+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:53:51+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean`\nTotal Lines: 537\nTotal Bytes: 27292\nShowing lines 440 to 475\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n440: def HasEdgeCover (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n441:   ∀ v : V, ∃ u : V, G.Adj v u\n442: \n443: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n444:     Decidable (HasEdgeCover G) := by unfold HasEdgeCover; infer_instance\n445: \n446: /- ── Roman domination ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -/\n447: \n448: /-- `f : V → {0,1,2}` is a Roman dominating function when every vertex labelled\n449: `0` has a neighbour labelled `2`.\n450: \n451: graphcalc solves this as a MIP over binaries `x, y` with `f = x + 2y`,\n452: constraint `x v + y v + Σ_{u ∈ N(v)} y u ≥ 1` and exclusion `x v + y v ≤ 1`;\n453: that is exactly the condition below. -/\n454: def IsRomanDominatingFunction (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n455:     (f : V → Fin 3) : Prop := ∀ v : V, f v = 0 → ∃ u : V, G.Adj v u ∧ f u = 2\n456: \n457: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (f : V → Fin 3) :\n458:     Decidable (IsRomanDominatingFunction G f) := by\n459:   unfold IsRomanDominatingFunction; infer_instance\n460: \n461: def romanWeight (f : V → Fin 3) : ℕ := ∑ v : V, (f v : ℕ)\n462: \n463: /-- Total, not partial: the constant function `2` is always Roman dominating\n464: (no vertex is labelled `0`), so a witness always exists. -/\n465: def romanDominationNumber (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ :=\n466:   (((univ.filter (IsRomanDominatingFunction G)).image romanWeight).min).getD 0\n467: \n468: /- ── double Roman domination ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -/\n469: \n470: /-- `f : V → {0,1,2,3}` is double Roman dominating when a vertex labelled `0`\n471: has either a neighbour labelled `3` or two neighbours labelled `2`, and a vertex\n472: labelled `1` has a neighbour labelled `≥ 2`.\n473: \n474: The first clause is written as graphcalc's linearisation\n475: `0.5·|N₂(v)| + |N₃(v)| ≥ 1`, but as two existentials rather than as\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
{"step_index":1469,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:53:51Z","thinking":"**Evaluating Roman Domination Number**\n\nConsidering the definitions of Roman domination number and radius of a graph, a hypothesis emerges: whether `radius G + 1 ≤ romanDominationNumber G` is always true. A comprehensive test plan is needed. The plan is to create a Python script to check with random graphs, special graphs, and trees. This will help understand if a general relationship holds.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python3 -c \"\nimport networkx as nx\n\ndef roman_domination_number(G):\n    # compute via ILP or brute force\n    import scipy.optimize as opt\n    import numpy as np\n    n = G.number_of_nodes()\n    nodes = list(G.nodes())\n    node_idx = {nodes[i]: i for i in range(n)}\n    # variables: x_v in {0, 1} (weight 1), y_v in {0, 1} (weight 2)\n    # total weight = sum(x_v + 2*y_v)\n    # constraints:\n    # x_v + y_v <= 1\n    # x_v + y_v + sum_{u in N(v)} y_u >= 1  => -x_v - y_v - sum y_u <= -1\n    c = np.array([1]*n + [2]*n)\n    A_ub = []\n    b_ub = []\n    for i in range(n):\n        # x_i + y_i <= 1\n        row = [0]*(2*n)\n        row[i] = 1\n        row[n+i] = 1\n        A_ub.append(row)\n        b_ub.append(1)\n    for v in nodes:\n        i = node_idx[v]\n        row = [0]*(2*n)\n        row[i] = -1\n        row[n+i] = -1\n        for u in G.neighbors(v):\n            row[n+node_idx[u]] = -1\n        A_ub.append(row)\n        b_ub.append(-1)\n    integrality = [1]*(2*n)\n    bounds = [(0, 1)]*(2*n)\n    res = opt.milp(c=c, integrality=integrality, bounds=opt.Bounds([0]*(2*n), [1]*(2*n)),\n                   constraints=opt.LinearConstraint(A_ub, -np.inf, b_ub))\n    return int(round(res.fun))\n\nprint('Testing path graphs...')\nfor n in range(2, 20):\n    G = nx.path_graph(n)\n    r = nx.radius(G)\n    gamma_R = roman_domination_number(G)\n    if r + 1 > gamma_R:\n        print(f'Counterexample P_{n}: r={r}, gamma_R={gamma_R}')\n    assert r + 1 <= gamma_R\n\nprint('Testing cycle graphs...')\nfor n in range(3, 20):\n    G = nx.cycle_graph(n)\n    r = nx.radius(G)\n    gamma_R = roman_domination_number(G)\n    if r + 1 > gamma_R:\n        print(f'Counterexample C_{n}: r={r}, gamma_R={gamma_R}')\n    assert r + 1 <= gamma_R\n\nprint('All paths and cycles passed!')\n\"","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9","RunPersistent":false,"WaitMsBeforeAsync":10000,"toolAction":"Testing paths and cycles in Python","toolSummary":"Test paths and cycles"}}]}
