{"step_index":1483,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:54:42Z","thinking":"**Verifying Roman Domination Inequality**\n\nConsidering paths, a hypothesis: the integer part of *n*/2 plus one is less than or equal to the ceiling of 2*n*/3. The Roman domination number of a path, *P*n, is known as the ceiling of 2*n*/3. Testing for several *n* values confirms this hypothesis seems valid so far.\n\n\n**Bounding Graph Radius**\n\nExamining path graphs' maximization of radius, the maximum radius for a connected graph of *n* vertices is tested. The diameter of such a graph is at most *n*-1, implying a radius of at most the floor of *n*/2. Adding edges can only reduce eccentricity, therefore the radius of a graph is at most that of a spanning tree, which is bounded by the floor of *n*/2.\n\nVerifying the bound with cycles, the radius of *Cn* is confirmed as consistent with the bound.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python3 -c \"\nimport networkx as nx\n# test all connected graphs up to n=8\nimport itertools\n\nfor n in range(2, 9):\n    # test random connected graphs\n    for _ in range(100):\n        G = nx.erdos_renyi_graph(n, 0.4)\n        if nx.is_connected(G):\n            assert nx.radius(G) <= n // 2\nprint('Radius <= n // 2 holds for all tested graphs!')\n\"","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9","RunPersistent":false,"WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Checking radius <= n // 2","toolSummary":"Check radius <= n // 2"}}]}
